          %h2#OriginAndRelatedConcepts Origin and relation to other concepts
          %p We discuss the general abstract structures of which Chern-Simons elements are presentations and how they are related to other structures.
          %p
            The term
            %em Chern-Simons element
            alludes to the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern-Simons form
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#PresentationForChernWeil
            As presentations for the
            = succeed "-Chern-Weil" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            We explain here briefly how Chern-Simons elements provide a
            %em presentation
            of a generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} Chern-Weil homomorphism
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            theory – in the sense in which
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentations
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
          %p
            To warm up, we start with considering a traditional setup of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            theory. Recall that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            dnoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
