                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in W(\mathfrak{a}) = \Omega^\bullet(X)
            as a binary invariant polynomial in degree 2.
          %p
            The Chern-Simons element is the canonical 1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            which in local coordinates is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = p_i d q^i
          %p The corresponding action functional on the line
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi ℝ
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{\mathbb{R}} \gamma^* (p_i\, d q^i)
          %p is the familiar term for the action functional of the particle (missing the kinetic term, which makes it “topological”).
